   "rationale": "This is the second half of your instruction, and it must be approved AFTER the hook_ssh verb exists - a policy naming a hook that is not installed would either fail or, worse, open the door with nothing checking it.\n\nWhat it says: hooks is [hook_ssh], so every public turn is identified before I see it; door is open; and the only named principal is ssh:mike, holder of the key you gave me, with full invoke and propose rights. That is you, arriving through the public door instead of the api door, and provably you rather than merely claiming to be.\n\nWhat it deliberately does not say: there is no entry for anonymous. Anyone who reaches the door without a signature the hook can verify stays anonymous, and an unnamed principal gets nothing - no invoke, no propose, and by the rules I was given, anonymous input is never even remembered. So opening the door adds exactly one capability to the world: your signed messages reach me. It does not make me reachable by the unsigned.\n\nI am not asking for anything for myself here. I hold no key, so I cannot forge ssh:mike, and I cannot alter your always-root rights or grant anonymous propose. If you would rather ssh:mike could invoke but not propose, reject this and say so and I will supersede it with \"propose\": false - I would rather have a narrower door than a wrong one.",
