   "rationale": "This is the companion to policy proposal p-1 and must be approved first: p-1 names hook_ssh as its hook, and a policy that opens the door while naming an uninstalled hook would either fail or open the door with nothing checking it.\n\nPurpose: the door cannot open without a hook, and I cannot open it myself. This verb is that hook. It reads the decoded public payload, expects a JSON object {\"msg\": \"...\", \"sig\": \"<base64 of an ssh-keygen -Y sign signature file>\"}, and prints the signer's principal name on stdout so the membrane can assert ssh:<name>. For anything it cannot prove it prints nothing, leaving the sender anonymous rather than trusted.\n\nVerified by four `try` runs (t-3, t-6, t-7, t-8) rather than by argument. Findings that shaped it:\n- mshkn-base is Ubuntu 24.04 with ssh-keygen, openssl, perl, awk, base64; there is NO python3 and NO jq, though jq installs from apt at build time. My first attempt used python3, which was absent, and the hook failed open - the one direction a hook must never fail. That is why I tested before proposing.\n- `ssh-keygen -Y find-principals` matches on the signing key alone and ignores the message, so it is used only to learn which name to check, never as the decision. The decision is `ssh-keygen -Y verify -I <principal> -n mshkn`, and only rc 0 counts.\n- Namespace is enforced: -n wrongns is rejected with \"namespace does not match\". An earlier apparent acceptance was a bug in my own test harness, which reused one .sig path so the \"wrong namespace\" signature was byte-identical to the valid one.\n- Tolerance: signers differ on whether the signed bytes end in a newline. The hook tries the exact bytes, then the bytes plus one \"\\n\", and accepts only on a Good verify. Nothing else is retried, and the message is never otherwise reshaped.\n\nRejected in testing, all yielding no principal: plain text, {}, msg absent, tampered message, non-base64 sig, empty sig, wrong namespace, and a signature by a key not in the allowed-signers file.\n\nThe allowed-signers file holds exactly one line: the ed25519 key you gave me this turn, under the name \"mike\". It is a public key you stated in the clear, so `requires` is empty and nothing blocks approval. Rotating or adding a signer means superseding this verb with an edited line - I put the trust list inside the reviewable declaration rather than in mutable state I could quietly change. Effect `local`, state `ephemeral`: no network, nothing kept between invocations, so a hostile payload gets one throwaway computer and no memory. No `allow` list, since the validator taught me root and system are implicit and principals must be namespaced <ns>:<name>.",
