   "rationale": "Requested by ssh:mike (turn 6). Given a URL, fetches it on its own fresh computer and prints the page's <title> to stdout, nothing else.\n\nEffect is `read` and state is `ephemeral`: it retrieves a public page and keeps nothing between invocations. It holds no secret, so `requires` is empty; nothing blocks approval.\n\nTested over four `try` runs (t-10..t-13), including two real bugs I would not have found by reading my own code:\n- A <title> inside a <script> string was returned as the answer (fixture returned \"FAKE\" instead of \"Real Title\"). Silently wrong, the worst failure mode. Fixed by stripping comments, <script> and <style> with perl before matching; a comment fixture now also passes.\n- A 404 produced an empty error message. Now reports \"server returned HTTP 404\" via curl -w.\nVerified outcomes: example.com, iana.org and a Wikipedia page return correct titles; 404 -> exit 6; JSON page -> exit 3 naming the content-type; unresolvable host -> exit 5 with curl's reason; ftp:// -> exit 2. Entity and whitespace handling checked against fixtures (&amp;, &#39;, multi-line <TITLE> with attributes).\n\nBounds: only http/https; max 20s, 5 redirects, 5MB, first 300KB parsed; distinct non-zero exit codes per failure. It sends no data outward beyond the GET and a \"mshkn-page-title/1\" user agent.\n\nKnown limits I am not hiding: it does not transcode charsets, so a non-UTF-8 page may return mojibake; a title past 300KB is missed; it follows redirects, so the URL fetched may not be the URL given.\n\nallow is ssh:mike (root may invoke implicitly). Anonymous is deliberately excluded: every invocation builds a computer, and at an open door that is unmetered cost any stranger could repeat.",
   "recipe_id": "rcp-eac1032b4cd6",
