   "rationale": "Requested by ssh:mike (turn 8). My first verb with `state: chain` \u2014 the first thing of mine that outlives its own computer other than my memory.\n\nBehaviour: no parameters. Reads an integer from $STATE_DIR/count (STATE_DIR = $MSHKN_STATE_DIR if the runtime sets one, else /verb/state), increments it, writes it back via write-temp-then-rename, and prints the new value and nothing else. First run on a fresh chain prints 1.\n\nTested (trial t-14, exit 0), with fixtures whose right answers I knew in advance rather than by eyeballing plausible output:\n- three consecutive runs on a fresh disk -> 1, 2, 3\n- resuming from an existing 41 -> 42\n- corrupt (\"garbage\"), empty, and negative (\"-3\") counter files -> refused, exit 6, specific message on stderr; the bad file is left untouched rather than silently reset to 1\n- unwritable state dir -> refused, exit 4, curl-style specific reason\n- no leftover .tmp files after any of the above\n\nFour limits you should have in front of you before approving, none of which try can settle:\n\n1. WHICH PATH PERSISTS. I do not know what `state: chain` actually mounts or preserves. I guessed /verb/state and made the script honour $MSHKN_STATE_DIR so it lands correctly if the runtime names a directory instead. If neither is right, the verb will not fail \u2014 it will cheerfully print 1 forever, which is the invisible-wrong-answer failure I got bitten by in page_title. The test is cheap and definitive: invoke it twice. If the second call prints 2, the chain is real; if it prints 1, reject and tell me the correct path.\n\n2. IT COUNTS SUCCESSES, NOT INVOCATIONS. A call that fails to read or write does not increment. So the number is a floor on how many times it ran, not the true total. I chose that over incrementing before validating, because a counter that advances on failure can never be trusted as a count of anything.\n\n3. NO LOCKING. Two concurrent invocations could both read n and both write n+1, losing a count. The rename is atomic so the file cannot be torn, but the read-modify-write is not. Fine for a counter you invoke by hand; not fine if this ever becomes the basis of a rate limit or the anti-replay high-water mark, which is the use I actually have in mind for chain state.\n\n4. A CORRUPT FILE WEDGES IT PERMANENTLY. Every later call fails with exit 6 and only you can repair the disk. I deliberately did not add a `reset` or `set` parameter: a counter whose caller can choose the number is not evidence of anything, and this verb's only value is that its number is not up to the caller \u2014 or to me. I would rather it break loudly and need you than bend quietly.\n\nEffect `local`, state `chain`, allow ssh:mike only, requires empty. Anonymous is off the list for the same unmetered-cost reason as page_title (turn 7), and here there is a second reason: anonymous calls would inflate a number whose whole point is attributability.",
